\details{
Brand scales (\code{"blue"}, \code{"gray"}, \code{"stone"}, \code{"teal"}, \code{"green"},
\code{"orange"}, \code{"red"}, \code{"purple"}) are nine-step ramps running light to dark,
named by shade. Position and shade are aligned by construction, so
\code{ekio_pal("blue")[7]} and \code{ekio_pal("blue")["700"]} are the same color.

Subsetting drops the names. A color picked out of a scale goes straight
into \code{ggplot2::scale_color_manual()}, which reads names as data levels.
The whole palette keeps its names for \code{\link[=ekio_text_on]{ekio_text_on()}} and for lookup.

The named \code{"ekio_brand"} palette contains colors for EKIO identity assets.
It is not intended as a general-purpose data visualization palette.

The \code{"academy"} palettes, \code{"yoro_blue"} and \code{"yoro_green"}, are for EKIO
Academy tutorials and books. They come from Hokusai's \emph{Yoro Waterfall in
Mino Province} and are tuned for \code{theme_ekio(background = "cold")}. Each is
a five-step ramp, light to dark, and the two share one lightness per step.
Steps 4 and 5 are dark enough for text on the cold surface.

\code{"full_light"} and \code{"spectrum_light"} are lighter categorical palettes for
filled marks such as bars, boxplots, and areas. \code{"full_light"} keeps the
order of \code{"full"}, so neighboring colors differ in hue. \code{"spectrum_light"}
follows the color wheel from blue to purple, which suits ordered
categories.

\code{"gold"} is an accent rather than a scale: three colors named \code{"light"},
\code{"mid"} and \code{"deep"}, because a nine-step gold ramp turns brown at the
dark end. They sit on the same lightness rungs as scale shades 300, 400
and 500.

\code{"accent_blue"} and \code{"accent_orange"} put one main color before a sequence
of receding grays. They return four colors by default; \code{n} can be set from
2 to 6 to match the number of series while keeping the main color first.
